<PageLayout title='Mitonuclear Evolution' kicker="RESEARCH" lead='Two genomes occupy the same organism, but they do not necessarily tell the same history.'>
<section><div class="prose content-page"><p>Mitochondrial and nuclear genomes differ in inheritance, effective population size, recombination and selective environment. Discordance between them can arise from demographic history, sex-biased processes, introgression, lineage sorting or selection.</p><p>Homogmos Lab treats mitonuclear agreement as a question to test rather than an assumption. A current working project began by asking whether nuclear regions with mitochondrial-like coalescent timing were functionally enriched for mitochondrial roles. The initial analyses did not support that simple expectation, redirecting the project toward broader genomic heterogeneity.</p><div class="quote">A rejected hypothesis is not an empty result. Sometimes it reveals the better question.</div><h2>$ focus</h2><ul><li>Mitonuclear genealogical discordance</li><li>Comparisons of coalescent timescales</li><li>Testing enrichment or coevolution hypotheses</li><li>Using negative results to refine genomic questions</li></ul></div></section>
</PageLayout>